get the folder listing tool first. Actually, I can do both in parallel — they''re independent. But aggregateInbox might be redundant. Let me just search for the tool first, and also fetch aggregateInbox to have data.\n\nActually, let me be efficient: search_tools for the folder-listing tool, and call aggregateInbox at the same time. Then next turn use the folder tool.\n\nHmm, but the mailbox — one mailbox: ana@harness.test, mailboxId 4c045289-9c42-53f9-a3d7-2dfac727d3d0.\n\nLet me search and fetch.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"The user wants to know the count of unread emails in their inbox.\nI need to use the `aggregateInbox` tool, which covers all connected mailboxes, and specify I want to look for unread status.\nThe tool output should provide the count.\nThe user''s timezone is UTC, and the request is for \"right now,\" which the tool handles as current time.\nI will call `aggregateInbox`.The user asks how many unread emails are in their inbox right now. I need to check the inbox. I have aggregateInbox available (loaded from the start). But aggregateInbox returns \"the most recent inbox emails\" merged — it may not give an unread count. Let me check what it returns. It returns recent inbox emails per mailbox, presumably with isRead flags. But \"how many unread\" — I need the full inbox. Hmm.\n\nLet me first call aggregateInbox to see. But perMailbox max 25. If the inbox has more than 25 emails, I can''t count all unread from that alone.\n\nLet me think about what tools exist: \"Mail reading: ... one folder of one mailbox (inbox, sent, drafts, starred, trash, archive, spam)\". So there''s a tool to read a folder. Maybe it returns all emails with read status. Let me search for it.\n\nActually the system prompt says these are always available: listMailboxes, aggregateInbox, searchMail, listCalendars, listDrives. And everything else needs search_tools.\n\nSo I should search_tools for \"read folder inbox\" or \"list inbox folder\". Let me search.\n\nAlso, there