 top-level listings for all 7 drives. Let me compare.\n\nDrives 1 (sftp c92b), 2 (ftp 66e4), 4 (ftp 6b8c), 5 (webdav 1a17): top level = preview/, welcome.txt (31 bytes), rebrand/ — 3 entries each. Same names.\n\nDrive 3 (ftp 7966, scoped): only rebrand/ — 1 entry. Because scoped to folders, we only see rebrand/. So it may have more, but hidden. Also possibly the scoping means we only see the allowed folder. So we cannot conclude it''s a subset — it''s a visibility limit. But at least \"rebrand\" is present in all.\n\nDrives 6 (s3 4258) and 7 (s3 a8ca): top level = edge/, preview/, rebrand/, welcome.txt — 4 entries. So they have an extra \"edge/\" folder.\n\nSo: not all the same. Six of seven share preview/, rebrand/, welcome.txt; the two S3 drives additionally have edge/. Drive 3 shows only rebrand/ (folder-scoped, so can''t tell).\n\nNow the question \"Do all of those drives have the same files in them?\" — I should verify deeper: are the contents of preview/ and rebrand/ the same? Let me look inside to give a real answer. Let me list preview/ and rebrand/ on a couple of drives, plus edge/ on the S3 ones.\n\nLet me check contents:\n- preview/ in drives 1,2,4,5,6,7\n- rebrand/ in drives 1,2,3,4,5,6,7\n- edge/ in drives 6,7\n\nThat''s a lot of calls but doable in one block. Let me do it — it''s worth it to answer accurately.\n\nActually, to be efficient: list preview/ and rebrand/ for each drive, and edge/ for the two S3.\n\nLet me issue calls. folderId for preview = \"preview/\", rebrand = \"rebrand/\", edge = \"edge/\".\n\nNote fileId values include trailing slash, e.g., \"preview/\". Let me use them as given.\n\nCalls:\n1. listFolder(c92b, preview/)\n2. listFolder(c92b, rebrand/)\n3. listFolder(66e4, preview/)\n4. listFolder(66e4, rebrand/)\n5. listFolder(7966, rebrand/)\n6. listFolder(6b8c, preview/)\n7. listFolder(6b8c, rebrand/)\n8. listFolder(1a17, preview/)\n9. listFolder(1a17, rebrand/)\n10. listFolder(4258, preview/)\n11. listFolder(4258, rebrand/)\n12. listFolder(4258, edge/)\n13. listFolder(a8ca, preview/)\n14. listFolder(a8ca, rebrand/)\n15. listFolder(a8ca, edge/)\n\nThat''s 15 calls. Maybe a bit many but fine. Alternatively I could just report the top-level difference and note I can go deeper. But the user asks specifically \"do all drives have the same files\" — a comparison. Let me do a reasonable check: preview/ and rebrand/ contents across drives.\n\nLet me do it in one block.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"The user asked if all 7 drives have the same files.\nI have performed `listFolder` on all 7 drives.\n\nReviewing the results:\nDrive 1 (c92b4299...): preview/, rebrand/, welcome.txt\nDrive 2 (66e47377...): preview/, rebrand/, welcome.txt\nDrive 3 (7966bf69...): rebrand/\nDrive 4 (6b8c0a7c...): preview/, rebrand/, welcome.txt\nDrive 5 (1a17bf00...): preview/, rebrand/, welcome.txt\nDrive 6 (4258fe0c...): edge/, preview/, rebrand/, welcome.txt\nDrive 7 (a8caddaf...): edge/, preview/, rebrand/, welcome.txt\n\nConclusion: No, they do not have the same files.\n\nDifferences:\n1. Drive 3 is missing several key folders and files present in others (e.g., ''preview/'', ''welcome.txt''). This is likely because it is a folder-scoped drive.\n2. Drives 6 and 7 have an additional top-level folder called ''edge/''.\n3. Drives 1, 2, 4, and 5 have a similar structure (preview, rebrand, welcome.txt), but Drive 6 and 7 are slightly different (they have ''edge/'' and the file type for welcome.txt is `null` while others are `text/plain`).\n\nI must communicate clearly that they are not identical, detail the differences, and explain that while some basic files (`welcome.txt`) are present, the overall structures and contents vary significantly.Now I have the top-level listings for all 7 drives. Let me compare.\n\nDrives 1 (sftp c92b), 2 (ftp 66e4), 4 (ftp 6b8c), 5 (webdav 1a17): top level = preview/, welcome.txt (31 bytes), rebrand/ — 3 entries each. Same names.\n\nDrive 3 (ftp 7966, scoped): only rebrand/ — 1 entry. Because