ferences (Recap):**\n*   Drives 6 (4258) and 7 (a8ca) contain an `edge/` directory. Drives 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 do not show this top-level directory (or the call didn''t target it).\n*   Drive 3 (7966) is folder-scoped and only shows `rebrand/` at the root, limiting comparison.\n\n**2. Deep Content Comparison (`preview/`):**\n*   All drives that have a visible `preview/` folder (Drives 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7) share an identical internal structure: `fallback/`, `text/`, `archive/`, `data/`, `docs/`, `audio/`, `image/`.\n*   Therefore, the *folder structure* within `preview/` is consistent across the non-scoped drives.\n\n**3. Deep Content Comparison (`rebrand/`):**\n*   All drives that have a visible `rebrand/` folder (Drives 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7) contain the file `brief.txt` (size 63 bytes), although modification dates vary or are null.\n*   The immediate contents of `rebrand/` appear to be the same (just `brief.txt`) across all drives checked.\n\n**4. Deep Content Comparison (`edge/` - Drives 6 & 7):**\n*   Drives 6 (4258) and 7 (a8ca) contain an `edge/` folder with an identical structure: 15 entries, including many subfolders (`big/`, `case/`, `docs/`, etc.) and specific files (`empty.txt`, `no-content-type.bin`, `one-byte`).\n\n**Conclusion Synthesis:**\nWhile the *folders* and *specific files* found in the main comparison points (`preview/`, `rebrand/`, `edge/`) are structurally identical where they appear, the overall composition is *not* the same because:\n1. Drive 3 is visibility limited (scoped drive).\n2. Drives 6 and 7 have the entire `edge/` directory, which is absent (or not visible) in the others.\n\nI will state clearly that they are not identical due to the differing top-level structure, but I will also point out the high degree of internal similarity for the common subfolders.Compare findings:\n\nSix drives (1 SFTP, 2 FTP, 4 FTP, 5 WebDAV, 6 S3, 7 S3) share: preview/ (with 7 subfolders: archive, audio, data, docs, fallback, image, text), rebrand/brief.txt (63 byte