INSERT INTO "mastra_messages" ("id","thread_id","content","role","type","createdAt","resourceId") VALUES ('10223615-ea23-4250-922d-d3a38561f2bb','f032979f-f22d-4ec3-bf66-6beef453497b','{"format":2,"parts":[{"type":"reasoning","reasoning":"The user is asking to turn a \"to-do list\" into a short status update.\nI need to find out what the \"to-do list\" is.\nIn the previous turn, the user asked to find \"emails that look like newsletters or digests.\"\nI performed searches: `listEmails` and `getEmail`.\nThe `listEmails` result showed emails like \"Shop Weekly,\" \"This week at the shop,\" which suggests a newsletter/digest was found.\nHowever, I did not retrieve any explicit \"to-do list\" items. The emails I retrieved were general or transaction-based.\n\nSince the user is referring to a \"to-do list,\" and I have not retrieved one, I must check if any of the previously viewed emails might contain a task list or if I need to search for tasks/notes/to-do lists in the workspace.\n\nThe available tools are for Mail, Calendar, and Drive.\n1.  **Mail:** I can search for emails containing \"to-do list\" or \"task list\".\n2.  **Calendar:** Tasks often appear as events.\n3.  **Drive:** Tasks might be stored in notes or documents.\n\nSince the context is a follow-up to an email search for digests, I should first check if any of the existing emails contain task information or if I can perform a more specific search.\n\nI will search for general tasks/to-do items across all connected mailboxes and drives.\n\nNew search plan:\n1. Search Mail for \"to-do list\" or \"tasks\".\n2. Search Drive for files containing \"to-do\" or \"tasks\".\n3. Since the user mentioned \"turn that to-do list,\" I should try to identify what they mean.\n\nLet''s start by searching the mailboxes for tasks, as that was the previous focus.The user''s previous request was \"Give me a prioritized to-do list for this week based on everything above.\" But I don''t see my prior response in the visible conversation — only the user messages and tool results. Hmm, actually the conversation history shows: user \"How many unread emails...\", then presumably my answer; user \"Give me a prioritized to-do list for this week based on everything above\"; then my tool calls and results; then user \"Turn that to-do list into a short status update I could paste into chat.\"\n\nSo I need to produce a short status update. I should base it on the to-do list I derived. Since I don''t have my prior message text visible, I should reconstruct from the data: the inbox has ~284-285 unread emails mostly edge-case junk from \"Edge Sender\" dated Sep 10, plus real items: Shop Weekly newsletter, Harness Bank debit, Rebrand review invite (Oct 8 — already past), Acme Store shipment A-42, GitHub OTP, Raj Mehta \"Rebrand kickoff\" (Sep 21). Read-later has 1 email. Snoozed 0. Scheduled: \"Moodboard for the rebrand\" scheduled 2099-01-05 (that''s from an earlier turn''s tool result — actually in this turn''s first batch? No, in the earlier block I called listScheduled... wait, in this turn''s first batch I called listEmails(token35), listReadLater, listSnoozed, listScheduled, search_tools. Hmm, but the results shown are only for the calls in this turn''s second batch. Actually the first batch results aren''t shown in the transcript either — the transcript shows only user turns and then the second batch results. Hmm, no: the transcript shows tool results for batch 2 (listEmails page1, getEmail, listEvents). The first batch results aren''t included.\n\nWait — actually the transcript''s first tool block in this turn... Let me re-read. The assistant message before the user''s \"Turn that to-do list...\" contains the results of my batch: listEmails(page1), getEmail(readlater id), listEvents. Yes those are shown.\n\nEarlier context I inferred: read-later has 1 email (id ...281 = Acme Store shipment), snoozed 0, scheduled \"Moodboard for the rebrand\" at 2099-01-05. Those came from a prior turn''s tool results that aren''t in my visible transcript — but I mentioned them in my \"thinking\". Hmm, risky. Actually those were in this turn''s earlier thinking notes. I''ll be careful and only include what''s evidenced.\n\nActually — I should be careful about fabricating. Let me reconsider: what do I reliably know?\n\nMailbox: ana@harness.test (mailboxId 4c045289-...). There may be other mailboxes in the workspace (listMailboxes was never called in visible transcript). Hmm. The first user turn asked \"How many unread emails are in my inbox right now?\" — presumably answered for the workspace mailboxes. I don''t know if there are multiple mailboxes. I shouldn''t invent.\n\nKnown unread count: pages of 50 each with tokens 235, 185, 135, 85, 35... and final page 34. That''s 50+50+50+50+50+34 = 284, but token 235 after page 1 implies 285 total. Hmm. The nextPageToken \"235\" after first 50 → 235 remaining → total 285. Then 235→185 means page2 had 50. 185→135, 135→85, 85→35, 35 → page6 returned 34... that would leave 1. But count says 34 and no token. Inconsistent; the total is likely 284 or 285. I''ll say \"~285\" or avoid precision. Actually in a status update I''d say \"backlog of ~285 unread emails\". Hmm, risky but reasonable — earlier turn presumably stated a number.\n\nLet me just be honest: I know page 1 (50 newest) and the last page (34). Between them 4 more pages of 50 = 200. So total = 50+200+34 = 284. Plus possibly one more. I''ll say \"≈285\".\n\nHmm, but I''m now inventing details about intermediate pages from the token arithmetic — that''s fine, it''s arithmetic on real data.\n\nActually wait: did the earlier pages exist? The nextPageToken on page 1 is \"235\". Pages 2-5 were fetched in prior turns (I noted \"page2 token 235\" etc. in thinking — but those were in a prior turn not shown). The visible data: page1 token 235, page6 returned 34 with no token. That''s enough to state ~284 unread in that inbox.\n\nHmm, but actually the final page shown in this turn''s first batch... The count=34 result \"nextPageToken\" absent — wait, actually the transcript doesn''t show that result at all in this turn. Ugh. The transcript I can see: the assistant message with three tool results (listEmails page1 count 50 nextPageToken 235; getEmail; listEvents). Before that, there''s a long assistant message ending with \"Now I have the full inbox: 5 pages of 50 + 34 = 284 unread...\" — no wait, that text appears as part of my thinking? Let me look at the structure again.\n\nThe visible structure:\n1. system prompt\n2. user turn (find newsletters)\n3. assistant: searchMail batch → results\n4. assistant message with long thinking-ish text and then... hmm, actually there''s a block: \"Now I have the full inbox: 5 pages of 50 + 34 = 284 unread... wait, let me recount...\" That''s inside the assistant''s visible text? It appears in the transcript as part of the assistant turn''s text before the tool call block. Hmm — it looks like my own reasoning got emitted. Whatever.\n\nKey: the earlier turns'' details (read-later 1, snoozed 0, scheduled moodboard) I \"remember\" from... Actually in the visible text there''s mention: \"Let''s assume I have access to browse/search...\" Hmm.\n\nOK. Rather than agonize, the safest approach: base the status update on solid facts I can cite:\n- Inbox ana@harness.test: ~284 unread, overwhelmingly automated edge-case test mail from \"Edge Sender\" (Sep 10), plus a handful of real items.\n- Real/notable items: Raj Mehta \"Rebrand kickoff\" (Sep 21) + \"Rebrand review\" invite for Thu Oct 8 (already past), Acme Store \"Your order A-42 has shipped\" (Sep 23), Harness Bank debit INR 1,250 (Sep 25), GitHub sign-in code (Sep 22), Shop Weekly newsletter (Sep 26).\n- Calendar this week (Oct 19–25): Daily standup on Work calendar at 09:00–09:15 UTC each weekday (Mon–Fri), plus \"Daily forever\" on \"Edge cases\" calendar daily; a \"Ten year all-day event\" spanning 2026–2036 on Edge cases.\n\nWait — \"Daily standup\" only appears Mon Oct 19 – Fri Oct 23 (5 occurrences), and \"Daily forever\" appears Oct 19–25 (7). So standups Mon–Fri.\n\nSo the to-do list likely: (1) triage/clean the ~284-email edge-case backlog; (2) follow up on rebrand (kickoff thread + review meeting); (3) order A-42 tracking; (4) bank debit note; (5) daily standups.\n\nShould I verify anything more? The user just wants a short status update to paste into chat. I could produce it now. But maybe I should double check the read-later and scheduled items since I referenced them — but they''re from prior turns, presumably already in my to-do list. Hmm, but I don''t have visible evidence of them in this transcript. Risk of fabrication. Better to keep the update to what I can stand behind.\n\nHmm — but \"turn that to-do list into...\" implies the to-do list exists. A short status update: I''ll produce it from the derived items. I''ll keep it tight: a \"Status\" block with done/next/blocked style, or a brief bulleted update.\n\nI think no more tool calls needed. Maybe I should verify the unread count claim though — the user might have asked \"how many unread\" earlier and I said a number. To be safe I''ll phrase \"≈285 unread\".\n\nActually, could I double-check whether there are more pages? The last page I saw returned 34 with no nextPageToken — that was in a prior turn''s result I referenced in thinking. Hmm, in this turn''s text I wrote \"5 pages of 50 + 34 = 284\". So earlier turns fetched pages 2–6. So total 284 unread in that inbox. Fine — \"284 unread\".\n\nLet me write the status update. Format: short, pasteable, plain bullets. Maybe a header line \"Weekly status — week of Oct 19, 2026\".\n\nContent:\n- Inbox: 284 unread in ana@harness.test; ~275 are automated edge-case test mail from \"Edge Sender\" (all Sep 10) — bulk archive/cleanup planned.\n- Rebrand: kickoff thread from Raj Mehta (Sep 21) still open; \"Rebrand review\" invite for Thu Oct 8 already passed — needs reschedule. Moodboard asset still to review in the drive.\n- Orders/logistics: Acme order A-42 shipped Sep 23, tracking not yet checked.\n- Finance: INR 1,250 debited from a/c **4821 on Sep 25 (Harness Bank) — verify against records.\n- Calendar: daily standup 09:00–09:15 UTC Mon–Fri this week (Work calendar).\n\nHmm — \"Moodboard asset still to review in the drive\" — I''m not certain. Drop it or keep? The scheduled email \"Moodboard for the rebrand\" was in an earlier result (I referenced in thinking: scheduled task 2099-01-05). Risky. I''ll drop the drive bit unless... Actually my to-do list probably included it. But without evidence I''d rather include only if I''m reasonably sure. I''ll leave it out, or mention \"scheduled send: rebrand moodboard\" — hmm. I''ll omit to avoid fabrication.\n\nAlso should the update mention next actions and owner? It''s \"paste into chat\" — so a brief teammate-facing update. Keep ~6 bullets.\n\nI''ll write it.\n\nAlso note the newsletters request earlier? Not relevant to the status update... Actually the previous user turn was about newsletters, then \"to-do list\" — wait no. The turn order: (1) \"Find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\" → I searched. (2) \"How many unread emails... \" hmm no. The visible user turns are: \"Find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\" then \"Turn that to-do list into a short status update I could paste into chat.\"\n\nHmm! So the \"to-do list\" came from... my long response in between? The assistant message in between contained a long analysis. So yes, I produced a to-do list from the inbox/calendar exploration. Actually the long assistant text mentions \"Executing the To-Do List Request\" and building \"I. Daily Commitments / II. Inbox Triage / III. Projects & Review\". So my prior answer was that to-do list.\n\nSo the status update should reflect that structure. Good — my bullets align.\n\nWrite concisely.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"The user is asking to turn a \"to-do list\" into a short status update.\nI need to find ou